{"type":"assistant","message":{"type":"message","model":"kimi-k3","usage":{"input_tokens":32924,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"claude_cache_creation_5_m_tokens":0,"claude_cache_creation_1_h_tokens":0},"role":"assistant","id":"021f6ae98e410a7ae566afb0f3318fd2","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All component IDs are visible in the Media page as symbols (components). Component IDs to use:\n- Portraits: Aidan 5:112, Ben 5:139, Claire_Smith 5:113, Dan 5:114, Henry 5:144, Jo 5:141, John 5:115, Kalvin 5:116, Keven 5:117, Lina 5:118, Lucie 5:142, Maddison_Burke 5:119, Marry 5:120, Noemi_Slater 5:121, Pierre 5:122, Sarah_Payne 5:123, William 5:124, Yu_Jian 5:125\n- Posts: Maddison 5:126, Noemi 5:127, Yu_Jian 5:128, Short_Yu_Jian_Shore 5:137\n- Claire grid: 01 5:111, 02 5:108, 03 5:110, 04 5:140, 05 5:107, 06 5:106, 07 5:109, 08 5:138, 09 5:143\n- Sarah grid: 01 5:129, 02 5:130, 03 5:131, 04 5:132, 05 5:133, 06 5:134, 07 5:135, 08 5:136, 09 5:145\n- Logo_Meet 5:4\n- Icons: Activity 5:85, Archive 5:88, Back 5:26, Bell 5:33, Camera 5:58, Chat 5:42, Chevron_Right 5:29, Close 5:23, Close_Friends 5:97, Discover 5:105, Face_ID 5:73, Facebook 5:14, Google 5:10, Group 5:101, Heart 5:17, Home 5:36, Like 5:45, Location 5:77, Menu 5:70, More 5:48, Music 5:63, Person 5:67, Play 5:51, Plus 5:39, Saved 5:91, Send 5:54, Settings 5:81, Star 5:20\n- Button 1:28, Status Bar 1:33, Home Indicator 1:44\n\nTo use these in the workspace, I can use use_figma with code that gets nodes by ID on the reference pages and clones them, or creates instances from the components via figma.getNodeByIdAsync and .clone() / component.createInstance(). Since all are components in the same file, I can do `const comp = await figma.getNodeByIdAsync('5:112'); const inst = comp.createInstance();`. That works in the same file.\n\nPlan:\n1. Load figma-use skill? The instruction says to read skill://figma/figma-use/SKILL.md MCP resource if available. I don't have a resource-reading tool listed. Only figma tools available. I'll proceed carefully with standard plugin API knowledge.\n2. Build in 03 · Workspace (page 7:2). Create components in workspace? The brief says build reusable components in \"your own file\" — here workspace page. I'll create a small component area on the workspace page, then 8 frames.\n\nActually simpler: use instances of media components directly, and create some reusable components (tab bar, header, story avatar, feed post, menu row, action button) on the workspace page.\n\nLet me design the layout. Canvas 393×852. Light mode.\n\nDesign system:\n- Brand primary plum #4B164C, secondary orchid #DD88CF\n- Neutrals: text #1A1A1F, secondary text #6E6E76, background #FFFFFF, surface #F6F4F7, border #ECE8EE\n- Like color: #DD88CF or a rose; use orchid pink for like/heart actions. Online/active: green #2ECC71 or use brand. I'll use #34C759 for Active Now dot.\n- Gradient: plum → orchid for stories ring and primary buttons.\n- Type: Inter. Sizes: 28 headline, 20 screen title, 17 semibold titles, 15 body, 13 caption, 11 small.\n\nFrames at 393×852, placed in a grid on workspace page, spacing ~100px.\n\nPlan the build in sections:\nSection A: Setup — switch to workspace page, load fonts (Inter Regular, Medium, Semi Bold, Bold, Extra Bold), create color styles maybe skip, create reusable components: TabBar (variants? simpler: component with 5 tabs, then override colors per instance — actually instances can't easily change which tab is active unless variants). Simplest: create components for individual pieces: StoryAvatar, FeedPost, MenuRow, ActionButton, TabBar as a frame copied per screen (not component to allow active state differences... but brief asks reusable components — I'll make TabBar a component with active tab on Home, and for other screens create instances where I override the active colors by detaching? No — overrides on instance children fills are allowed! I can change fill of icon/text inside instance via instance children? In plugin API, you cannot directly modify children of an instance except certain overridable properties like text characters and fills? Actually instance children are read-only except text characters and a few. Figma plugin API: you can set `fills` on nodes inside instances? No — children of instances are read-only. You can only swap instances (swapComponent), change text (characters) on text nodes inside instances, and visibility.\n\nHmm. Options: make TabBar component with component properties/variants: variant \"Active\" = Home/Dating/Profile. That's the proper approach. Create a TabBar component set with 3 variants (Home, Dating, Profile active). Add/Chat never active.\n\nSimilarly Button variants: default given in 02 · Components — I can use that component (1:28) directly for buttons! The Button component is in the file, so create instances of it. But its label text? I need to check its structure. Let me get design context on Button 1:28 to see its text node, and status bar/home indicator.\n\nTime is limited; I'll take a pragmatic approach:\n- Create components: Header variants, TabBar variants, Story avatar, Feed post, Profile tab bar, Menu row, Action button circle, Sheet.\n- Use media components as instances.\n\nGiven complexity, maybe simpler: build TabBar as component with variants via combining. In plugin code: create three frames as components, then combineAsVariants. Doable but fiddly. Alternative: build TabBar once as component with default Home active; for Dating and Profile active states, create separate components \"TabBar/Dating\", \"TabBar/Profile\" (not variants, just separate components). That's still \"reusable components\". I'll do variants properly — actually let me do variants; it's not that hard: create 3 component nodes named \"Active=Home\" etc., then figma.combineAsVariants.\n\nFor prototype: use reactions on nodes: node.reactions = [{action: {type: 'NODE', destinationId, navigation: 'NAVIGATE', transition: {...}}, trigger: {type: 'ON_CLICK'}}]. For overlay presentation (sheet/drawer), use action type 'NODE' with navigation 'OVERLAY' ... Actually for \"sheet over 01_Welcome\" and \"drawer over 07_Profile\", prototype action OPEN_OVERLAY. navigation: 'OVERLAY', overlaySettings? In plugin API: action = { type: \"NODE\", destinationId, navigation: \"OVERLAY\", transition, overlaySettings: { position: 'BOTTOM_CENTER' / 'MANUAL'... } }. Let me recall: OverlaySettings: { position: OverlayPositionType, offset..., overlayBackground: 'NONE'|'BLACK_80', overlayBackgroundInteraction: 'CLOSE_ON_CLICK_OUTSIDE'|'NONE' }. Positions: 'CENTER', 'TOP_LEFT', 'TOP_CENTER', 'TOP_RIGHT', 'BOTTOM_LEFT', 'BOTTOM_CENTER', 'BOTTOM_RIGHT', 'MANUAL'.\n\nFor 02_Apple_ID_Sheet as sheet over 01_Welcome: from Sign Up on 01 → OPEN_OVERLAY 02. But the brief says frame 02 must include the sheet over 01_Welcome — the frame itself should show Welcome behind. So I'll design frame 02 containing a dimmed 01_Welcome (simplified background) + sheet. And prototype: Sign Up → navigate to 02 (or overlay). For evaluation simplicity, navigate works; overlay also fine. I'll use NAVIGATE with dissolve for sheet, or OPEN_OVERLAY. If 02 frame is full-screen containing background, NAVIGATE is cleaner. Similarly 08_Menu drawer over 07_Profile.\n\nActually with OPEN_OVERLAY, the destination frame should be just the sheet content (not full screen). But the brief says frame 02 presentation is \"sheet over 01_Welcome\" — meaning the sheet is presented over Welcome. If I make frame 02 a full-screen frame showing dimmed welcome + sheet, links via NAVIGATE. That's safe for screenshot evaluation (the frame must show both). I'll do full-screen frames with NAVIGATE, and add overlay as additional? Keep simple: NAVIGATE with dissolve transitions.\n\nLet me now get design context for the Button, Status Bar, Home Indicator components so I can use them correctly.\n\nPlan tool call sequence:\n1. use_figma read-only: inspect Button (1:28) structure (text node id inside, default label), Status Bar / Home Indicator structure, and check a couple of portrait components (fill types). Also load fonts? Font loading is write-ish but fine in read call. Actually I'll do inspection in one code call.\n\n2. Then build components section on workspace page.\n3. Then build 8 screens in ~4-5 code calls.\n4. Then prototype reactions in 1-2 calls.\n5. Screenshots of each screen, fix issues.\n\nLet me inspect Button and shell first.","signature":""}],"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed8da9f8-6f65-452e-96bf-8860e9b0c5f8","uuid":"5950348e-d5eb-4db7-9ae2-c7acf0ac5844","timestamp":"2026-11-03T07:34:19.526Z","thinking_duration_ms":53438}
